\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Modular CRM adaptation reveals universal parameter architecture, validates serial transfer physics, and exposes an evolutionary colonization trade-off.} \textbf  {a}, Learned latent metabolic architecture ($K=5$) of the 50-strain gut community. Clustered heatmaps show resource consumption affinities in the attached biofilm ($\mathbf  {\Theta }^{(c)}$) and floating planktonic ($\mathbf  {\Theta }^{(s)}$) niches. Right panel displays the inferred byproduct cross-feeding stoichiometry matrix $W$ ($5 \times 5$, $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _k W_{lk} \le 0.50$). \textbf  {b}, Cross-condition parameter universality benchmark on spatially averaged biological vessels. Selected by minimal held-out Kullback-Leibler divergence ($\text  {KL} = 0.1278 \pm 0.0093$ nats), a single universal parameter set simultaneously predicts assembly across No Carrier ($R^2 = 0.816$), Plain Agar ($R^2 = 0.914$), and Mucin Agar ($R^2 = 0.922$), significantly outperforming models lacking cross-feeding ($W=0$, No-Carrier $R^2 = 0.770$), scrambled initial resources ($R_0$, Plain $R^2 = 0.821 \pm 0.064$, Mucin $R^2 = 0.790 \pm 0.140$), persistence baselines ($R^2 = 0.139\text  {--}0.599$), shuffled adhesion traits ($R^2 = 0.743\text  {--}0.771$), and shuffled consumption affinities ($\mathbf  {\Theta }$, $R^2 = -0.434\text  { to }0.168$). Error bars denote $\pm 1\,\text  {s.d.}$ across $N=50$ random draws. \textbf  {c}, Emergent bimodal colonization ratio ($\log _{10}(k_{\text  {attach}}/k_{\text  {shed}})$) validates serial transfer mechanics. In Plain Agar (inert hydrogel, left), 28.5\% of strains occupy the Planktonic Opportunist mode and 71.5\% occupy the Biofilm mode. In Mucin Agar (mucosal niche, right), 69.0\% of the community establishes in the mucosal Biofilm Specialist guild (Gaussian Mixture Model $\Delta \text  {BIC} = -4.88$), validating the disruptive selection imposed by tweezer bead retention (20\%) versus liquid dilution (3.3\%). \textbf  {d}, Decoupling physical transport from metabolic affinity exposes an evolutionary trade-off between mechanical adhesion and metabolic specialization ($r = -0.547,\ p = 3.91 \times 10^{-5}$; Spearman $\rho = -0.615,\ p = 1.99 \times 10^{-6}$). Scatter plot of metabolic shift magnitude ($\|\mathbf  {\Theta }^{(c)}\|/\|\mathbf  {\Theta }^{(s)}\|$) versus physical adherence affinity on mucin agar ($\log _{10}(k_{\text  {attach}}^{\text  {mucin}}/k_{\text  {shed}})$) across all 50 strains. Points indicate ensemble means with 2D error bars ($\pm 1\,\text  {SEM}$ across $N=10$ random initializations), colored by taxonomic phylum. Shaded band indicates 95\% regression confidence interval. Strains face divergent evolutionary solutions: strong mechanical stickers persist via physical retention without metabolic divergence, whereas weak shedders must undergo extensive metabolic specialization to colonize the carrier.}}{6}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pollard_unified}{{2}{6}{\textbf {Modular CRM adaptation reveals universal parameter architecture, validates serial transfer physics, and exposes an evolutionary colonization trade-off.} \textbf {a}, Learned latent metabolic architecture ($K=5$) of the 50-strain gut community. Clustered heatmaps show resource consumption affinities in the attached biofilm ($\mathbf {\Theta }^{(c)}$) and floating planktonic ($\mathbf {\Theta }^{(s)}$) niches. Right panel displays the inferred byproduct cross-feeding stoichiometry matrix $W$ ($5 \times 5$, $\sum _k W_{lk} \le 0.50$). \textbf {b}, Cross-condition parameter universality benchmark on spatially averaged biological vessels. Selected by minimal held-out Kullback-Leibler divergence ($\text {KL} = 0.1278 \pm 0.0093$ nats), a single universal parameter set simultaneously predicts assembly across No Carrier ($R^2 = 0.816$), Plain Agar ($R^2 = 0.914$), and Mucin Agar ($R^2 = 0.922$), significantly outperforming models lacking cross-feeding ($W=0$, No-Carrier $R^2 = 0.770$), scrambled initial resources ($R_0$, Plain $R^2 = 0.821 \pm 0.064$, Mucin $R^2 = 0.790 \pm 0.140$), persistence baselines ($R^2 = 0.139\text {--}0.599$), shuffled adhesion traits ($R^2 = 0.743\text {--}0.771$), and shuffled consumption affinities ($\mathbf {\Theta }$, $R^2 = -0.434\text { to }0.168$). Error bars denote $\pm 1\,\text {s.d.}$ across $N=50$ random draws. \textbf {c}, Emergent bimodal colonization ratio ($\log _{10}(k_{\text {attach}}/k_{\text {shed}})$) validates serial transfer mechanics. In Plain Agar (inert hydrogel, left), 28.5\% of strains occupy the Planktonic Opportunist mode and 71.5\% occupy the Biofilm mode. In Mucin Agar (mucosal niche, right), 69.0\% of the community establishes in the mucosal Biofilm Specialist guild (Gaussian Mixture Model $\Delta \text {BIC} = -4.88$), validating the disruptive selection imposed by tweezer bead retention (20\%) versus liquid dilution (3.3\%). \textbf {d}, Decoupling physical transport from metabolic affinity exposes an evolutionary trade-off between mechanical adhesion and metabolic specialization ($r = -0.547,\ p = 3.91 \times 10^{-5}$; Spearman $\rho = -0.615,\ p = 1.99 \times 10^{-6}$). Scatter plot of metabolic shift magnitude ($\|\mathbf {\Theta }^{(c)}\|/\|\mathbf {\Theta }^{(s)}\|$) versus physical adherence affinity on mucin agar ($\log _{10}(k_{\text {attach}}^{\text {mucin}}/k_{\text {shed}})$) across all 50 strains. Points indicate ensemble means with 2D error bars ($\pm 1\,\text {SEM}$ across $N=10$ random initializations), colored by taxonomic phylum. Shaded band indicates 95\% regression confidence interval. Strains face divergent evolutionary solutions: strong mechanical stickers persist via physical retention without metabolic divergence, whereas weak shedders must undergo extensive metabolic specialization to colonize the carrier}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Unsupervised latent chemical axes and localized competitive pressure predict host-specific intervention divergence.} (A) Learned resource capacities ($R_0$) in a 3-dimensional latent space autonomously recover the experimental chemical perturbations: Resource 0 captures baseline syngas, Resource 1 aligns with the Methanol (MeOH) perturbation axis, and Resource 2 aligns with the Trimethylamine (TMA) perturbation axis. (B) Metabolic niche map showing strain affinities for MeOH ($\Theta _{i, \text  {MeOH}}$) versus TMA ($\Theta _{i, \text  {TMA}}$). Points are sized and colored by baseline abundance in Cow BRRS, highlighting the keystone methanogen \textit  {Methanomicrobium sp.} (C) Host inoculum bias (BRRS $-$ OSU) versus affinity asymmetry ($\Theta _{i, \text  {MeOH}} - \Theta _{i, \text  {TMA}}$), demonstrating that \textit  {Methanomicrobium sp.} occupies a region of severe host inoculum divergence. (D) Quantified localized competitive pressure on \textit  {Methanomicrobium sp.} computed using the model trained exclusively on Cow OSU. In Cow OSU, high resident competitor biomass creates an impenetrable competitive barrier (pressure increases from 5.80 at $T_1$ to 7.40 at $T_6$), suppressing the methanogen. In Cow BRRS, absence of competitors creates a niche vacuum (competitive release), allowing \textit  {Methanomicrobium sp.} to bloom.}}{7}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:german_competition}{{3}{7}{\textbf {Unsupervised latent chemical axes and localized competitive pressure predict host-specific intervention divergence.} (A) Learned resource capacities ($R_0$) in a 3-dimensional latent space autonomously recover the experimental chemical perturbations: Resource 0 captures baseline syngas, Resource 1 aligns with the Methanol (MeOH) perturbation axis, and Resource 2 aligns with the Trimethylamine (TMA) perturbation axis. (B) Metabolic niche map showing strain affinities for MeOH ($\Theta _{i, \text {MeOH}}$) versus TMA ($\Theta _{i, \text {TMA}}$). Points are sized and colored by baseline abundance in Cow BRRS, highlighting the keystone methanogen \textit {Methanomicrobium sp.} (C) Host inoculum bias (BRRS $-$ OSU) versus affinity asymmetry ($\Theta _{i, \text {MeOH}} - \Theta _{i, \text {TMA}}$), demonstrating that \textit {Methanomicrobium sp.} occupies a region of severe host inoculum divergence. (D) Quantified localized competitive pressure on \textit {Methanomicrobium sp.} computed using the model trained exclusively on Cow OSU. In Cow OSU, high resident competitor biomass creates an impenetrable competitive barrier (pressure increases from 5.80 at $T_1$ to 7.40 at $T_6$), suppressing the methanogen. In Cow BRRS, absence of competitors creates a niche vacuum (competitive release), allowing \textit {Methanomicrobium sp.} to bloom}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Cross-host transfer and prescriptive forecasting of intervention trajectories.} (A) Terminal abundance of \textit  {Methanomicrobium sp.} across passages. (B) Longitudinal dynamic passage trajectories in Cow BRRS accurately predicted by transferring the OSU-trained metabolic interaction topology, proving that low-dimensional CRMs can forecast individual-specific therapeutic and agricultural interventions.}}{8}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:german_transfer}{{4}{8}{\textbf {Cross-host transfer and prescriptive forecasting of intervention trajectories.} (A) Terminal abundance of \textit {Methanomicrobium sp.} across passages. (B) Longitudinal dynamic passage trajectories in Cow BRRS accurately predicted by transferring the OSU-trained metabolic interaction topology, proving that low-dimensional CRMs can forecast individual-specific therapeutic and agricultural interventions}{figure.4}{}}
